WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:01.968
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.369
We're here
with Allison Williams.

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:05.004
Arlo, I do want to ask before
we talk about the podcast.

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.606
Is he a performer as well?

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.475
Do you sense that,
having performers as parents?

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:11.577
-He's a little bit
of a performer in that

00:00:11.577 --> 00:00:13.012
he will sing occasionally.

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:15.048
He has a little guitar
that he likes to strum,

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:20.987
but he's very bossy about the
way I am during his performance.

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:23.756
-Interesting.
-Which I'm working very hard

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:26.926
to not allow hurt my feelings.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:28.594
-What does he need more of?
Yeah.

00:00:28.594 --> 00:00:31.097
-Well, he needs less of me,
just in general.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:32.731
[ Laughter ]

00:00:32.731 --> 00:00:34.167
But kind of specifically,

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:35.635
so sometimes I'm allowed
to sing with him

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:36.836
and then other people aren't.

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:38.337
Sometimes I'm excluded
from singing from him,

00:00:38.337 --> 00:00:41.006
but other people can't.
Sometimes I can't dance.

00:00:41.006 --> 00:00:43.977
Sometimes I'm allowed
to just dance and no singing.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:46.145
Sometimes I have to stay
perfectly still.

00:00:46.145 --> 00:00:47.847
Sometimes I'm not allowed
to look at him.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:49.849
-Okay.
-One might say I'm giving him

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.684
too much power.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:53.886
And that is why he will be
in therapy later.

00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:56.856
I owe him a reason
for therapy later in life.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.857
-Does he have a favorite song?

00:00:58.857 --> 00:01:00.326
Does he have a go-to?

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:02.595
-His favorite song is "Lover"
by Taylor Swift.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:05.664
-Well, a lot of...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:05.664 --> 00:01:09.035
-It's a really -- I can't say
I wasn't involved in that.

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:10.904
-They say it's a perfect song
for 2-year-olds.

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:13.539
-It's a perfect song for
a 2-year-old and his mom.

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:15.174
Another reason
to send him to therapy.

00:01:15.174 --> 00:01:19.412
-It's like, "And then she'd make
me do 'Lover' and...

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:22.382
And she wouldn't look at me."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:24.016
Alright, so this podcast,
this is fascinating.

00:01:24.016 --> 00:01:25.452
This is a true-crime podcast.

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:29.255
This is not a story I knew of,
but a lot of names in this,

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:31.591
in this story that I think
we're all aware of.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:34.093
Can you tell us a little bit
about Elma Sands and the story?

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:37.530
-So basically the way
I was introduced to it

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.833
is a woman named Allison Flom
who created and directed

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:42.335
and wrote the whole thing,

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:44.003
said I was a tour guide
in Manhattan,

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:45.337
and I discovered this well,

00:01:45.337 --> 00:01:47.707
in the basement of a store
in SoHo that was unmarked,

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:50.509
and I learned that it was
the site of a murder in 1799

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:52.979
during a pandemic.
And in that murder trial,

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:56.616
which was the first murder trial
with the new U.S. Constitution,

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:59.352
the two lawyers who were
co-counsel for the defense,

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:01.687
her murderer,
were Hamilton and Burr.

00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:03.323
And so there's like -- A gasp.

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:04.891
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:07.693
So there was -- there's a line
about it really quickly

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:09.796
in "Hamilton" where they say,
"Levi Weeks is innocent.

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:11.998
Call your first witness.
That's all you had to say."

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:14.868
And he is the person
that they're defending.

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:16.970
But there's no mention
of Elma in there.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.706
And so this goes back
through very flawed

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:23.642
court transcripts from the time
trying to reconstruct the trial

00:02:23.642 --> 00:02:25.712
with a kind of radio play,

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:28.915
true crime with modern language.

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:30.182
So it's a little bit
more accessible,

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:31.851
but it's just like
full of details

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:33.586
that are too crazy
to be believed,

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:36.256
like it was the first time
anyone used a stenographer.

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:40.426
And this stenographer was the
tutor of Philip, Hamilton's son.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.495
So we're guessing
it's not, like, super accurate.

00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:45.898
It has a lot of descriptions
of the prosecutor,

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:49.335
like being exhausted
and like haggard

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:50.637
and like Hamilton and Burr,

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:53.205
just these, like,
shining heroes of the case.

00:02:53.205 --> 00:02:54.840
But it's really interesting
to think about,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.710
like, this is the first trial
with a brand-new Constitution.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.946
They could have done
some like wild, creative things,

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:02.481
and they just baked
all of the old systems

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:03.949
right into it
from the beginning.

00:03:03.949 --> 00:03:07.286
It's just interesting
to delve back into that world

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:08.554
and then walk around New York

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:10.122
and realize that you're still
in that world.

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:12.091
SoHo was this area
called the Lispenard,

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.827
and there was the Manhattan well
in the middle of this meadow.

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:16.762
And it's just so hard
to imagine,

00:03:16.762 --> 00:03:18.665
and it's so close
at the same time.

00:03:18.665 --> 00:03:20.032
-Oh, it's a really cool project,

00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:21.868
and it's always just
the best to see you.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:23.469
Thank you so much
for coming back, Allison.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:24.503
-Aw, thank you for having me.

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:25.838
-You guys, that's
Allison Williams.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:29.308
"Erased: The Murder of
Elma Sands" is available

00:03:29.308 --> 00:03:31.176
on Apple Podcasts now.
It's great.

00:03:31.176 --> 00:03:32.745
-We'll be right back
with Fisher Stevens.

